== 5. Conflict as in Hypocrites

#strong[The fifth device that Satan has to keep souls in a sad, doubting, and questioning condition, is,]

BY suggesting to them that the conflict#footnote[John 8:44. The devil is a liar, and the father of it. “The devil’s breasts,” says Luther, “are very fruitful with lies.”] that is in them is not a conflict found only in saints, but such a conflict as is found in hypocrites and profane souls; when the truth is, there is as much difference between the conflict that is in them and that which is in wicked men as there is between light and darkness, between heaven and hell. And I will make the truth of this plain to you in the following particulars.

1\. The whole frame of a believer’s soul is against sin: understanding, will, and affections, all the powers and faculties#footnote[It was a good prayer of the man who said (#emph[Domine libera me a malo homine meipso]): “Lord, deliver me from an evil man, myself.” Augustine complains that men do not tame the beasts in their own bosoms. Rom 2:21, 22, 23.] of the soul, are in arms against sin. A covetous man may condemn covetousness, and yet the frame and bent of his heart may be toward it. A proud person may condemn pride, and yet the frame of his spirit may be toward it. The drunkard may condemn drunkenness, and yet the frame of his spirit may be toward it. A man may condemn stealing and lying, and yet the frame of his heart may be toward them. “You who preach against stealing, do you steal? You who forbid adultery, do you commit adultery? You who abhor idols, do you rob temples? You who boast in the law, do you dishonor God by breaking the law?” But a saint’s will is against sin: “I keep on doing the evil I do not want to do.” And his affections are against#footnote[Rom 7:19, 15.] it: “What I hate, I do.”

2\. A saint fights against sin universally,#footnote[Ps 119:104. “I hate every false way.” #emph[Sanethi,] from שנא, which signifies to hate with a deadly and irreconcilable hatred.] the least as well as the greatest, the most profitable and the most pleasing sin as well as those that are less pleasing and profitable. He will do battle with all, though he cannot conquer one as he should and as he would. He knows that all sin strikes at God’s holiness as well as at his own happiness, at God’s glory as well as at his soul’s comfort and peace.

He knows that all sin is hateful to God, and that all sins are traitors to the crown and dignity of the Lord Jesus. He looks on one sin and sees#footnote[He knows that all the parts of the old man have played, and do play, the part of a treacherous friend and a friendly traitor; therefore his heart strikes at all. “The greater the combat is, the greater will be the rewards that follow,” says Tertullian.] that it threw down Noah, the most righteous man in the world; and he looks on another sin and sees that it cast down Abraham, the greatest believer in the world; and he looks on another sin and sees that it threw down David, the best king in the world; and he looks on another sin and sees that it cast down Paul, the greatest apostle in the world. He sees that one sin threw down Samson, the strongest man in the world; another cast down Solomon, the wisest man in the world; another, Moses, the meekest man in the world; and another sin cast down Job, the most patient man in the world. And this raises a holy indignation in his soul against all, so that nothing can satisfy and content#footnote[True hatred is πρὸς γένη, against the whole kind. Plutarch reports of one who would not have his doubts resolved, because he would not lose the pleasure of seeking a resolution. So wicked men will not be rid of some sins, because they would not lose the seeming pleasure of sinning.] his soul but the destruction of all those lusts and vermin that vex and rack his righteous heart. It will not satisfy a gracious soul to see justice done on one sin; he cries out for justice on all. He would not have some crucified and others spared, but cries out, “Lord, crucify them all, crucify them all.” Oh, but the conflict that is in wicked men is partial. They frown on one sin and smile on another; they strike at some sins but stroke others; they thrust some out of doors but keep others close in their bosoms, as you may see in Jehu, Herod, Judas, Simon Magus, and Demas. Wicked men strike at gross sins, such as are against not only the law of God but the laws of nature and nations; but they make nothing of lesser sins, such as vain thoughts, idle words, sinful impulses,#footnote[John 3:20.] petty oaths, etc. They fight against those sins that fight against their honor, profits, pleasures, etc., but make a truce with those that are as right hands and right eyes to them, etc.

3\. The conflict that is in a saint against sin is maintained by spiritual arguments: by arguments drawn from#footnote[Though to be kept from sin brings comfort to us, yet for us to oppose sin from spiritual and heavenly arguments, and for God to pardon sin, brings most glory to God. 2 Cor 12:7, 8, 9.] the love of God, the honor of God, the sweetness of communion with God, and from the spiritual and heavenly blessings and privileges that are conferred on them by God; by arguments drawn from the blood of Christ, the glory of Christ, the eye of Christ, the kisses of Christ, and the intercession of Christ; and by arguments drawn from the earnest of the Spirit, the seal of the Spirit, the witness of the Spirit, the comforts of the Spirit. Oh, but the conflict that is in wicked men comes from low, carnal, and legalistic arguments, drawn from the eye, ear, or hand of man, or drawn from shame, hell, the curses of the law, etc.

4\. The conflict that is in saints is a#footnote[It was an excellent saying of Eusebius Emesenus: “Our fathers overcame the torments of the flames; let us overcome the fiery darts of vices.” Consider that the pleasure and sweetness that follow victory over sin are a thousand times beyond the seeming sweetness that is in sin.] constant conflict. Though sin and grace were not born together in the heart of a saint, and though they will not die together, yet while a believer lives they must be in conflict together. Paul had been converted about fourteen years when he cried out, “I see another law at work in my body, warring against the law of my mind and holding me captive to the law of sin.”

Pietro Candiano, one of the doges of Venice, died fighting against the Narentines with his weapons in his hands. So a saint lives fighting and dies fighting; he stands fighting and falls fighting, with his spiritual weapons in his hands. But the conflict that is in wicked men is inconstant. Now they fall out with sin, and the next moment they fall in with sin; now it is bitter, the next moment it is sweet. Now the sinner turns from his sin, and soon he turns back to wallowing in sin, as the pig does to wallowing in the mire. One hour you will find him#footnote[2 Pet 2:19, 20.] praying against sin, as if he feared it more than hell, and the next hour you will find him pursuing sin, as if there were no God to punish him, no justice to damn him, no hell to torment him.

5\. The conflict that is in the saints is in the same faculties: there is the judgment against the judgment, the mind against the mind, the will against the will, the affections against the affections;#footnote[A heathen could say that their soul is in a mutiny: a wicked man is not friends with himself; he and his conscience are at odds. Aristotle.] that is, the regenerate part against the unregenerate part, in all the parts of the soul. But in wicked men the conflict is not in the same faculties, but between the conscience and the will. The will of a sinner is bent strongly toward such and such sins; but conscience steps in and tells the sinner, “God has made me his deputy; he has given me power to hang and draw, to examine, scourge, judge, and condemn. If you do such and such wickedness, I will be your jailer and your tormentor. I do not bear the rod or the sword in vain,” says conscience. “If you sin, I will do my office, and then your life will be a hell.” And this raises a tumult in the soul.

6\. The conflict that is in the saints is a more blessed, successful, and prevailing conflict. A saint by his conflict with sin gains ground on his sin. “Those who belong to Christ” (says the apostle) “have#footnote[These two, grace and sin, are like two buckets at a well: when one is up, the other is down. They are like the two laurels at Rome: when one flourishes, the other withers. The more grace thrives in the soul, the more sin dies in the soul. 2 Tim 3:13. From wicked they grow to be very wicked, and from very wicked to be utterly wicked. Lactantius says of Lucian, #emph[Nec diis nec hominibus pepercit,] he spared neither God nor man. Mortification is a continued act; it is a daily dying to sin: “I die every day.” A crucified man will strive and struggle, yet in the eyes of the law, and in the account of all who see him, he is dead; it is just so with sin. Ps 59:11. “There is no such pleasure” (says Cyprian) “as to have overcome a pleasure offered, nor is there any greater conquest than that which is gained over a man’s corruptions.”] crucified the flesh with its passions and desires.” Christ puts his hand to it and helps them to lead captivity captive, and to set their feet on the necks of those lusts that once trampled on their souls and their comforts. As the house of Saul grew weaker and weaker, and the house of David grew stronger and stronger, so the Lord, by the revelations of his love and by the influences of his Spirit, causes grace, the noble part of a saint, to grow stronger and stronger, and corruption, like the house of Saul, to grow weaker and weaker. But sin in a wicked heart gains ground and grows stronger and stronger. For all his conflicts, his heart is more encouraged, emboldened, and hardened in a way of sin, as you may see in the Israelites, Pharaoh, Jehu, and Judas, who no doubt felt many strange conflicts, tumults, and mutinies in their souls when God spoke such bitter things against them and executed such justice on them.

But remember this by way of caution: though Christ has given sin its death wound (by his power, Spirit, death, and resurrection), yet it will die only a lingering death. As a man who is mortally wounded dies little by little, so does sin in the heart of a saint. The death of Christ on the cross was a lingering death; so the death of sin in the soul is a lingering death: now it dies a little, and then it dies a little more, #emph[etc.] As the psalmist says, “Do not kill them, or my people will forget. Scatter them by Your power, and bring them down, O Lord, our shield.” He would not have them utterly destroyed, but some remnants preserved as a memorial. So God deals with sin: it is wounded and brought down, but not wholly slain. Something is still left as a monument of divine grace, and to keep us humble, wakeful, and watchful, and so that our armor may always be kept on, and our weapons always in our hands. The best men’s souls in this life hang between the flesh and the spirit, as it were, like Muhammad’s tomb at Aleppo between two lodestones; like Erasmus, as the papists paint him, between#footnote[The Romans lost many a battle, and yet in the end were conquerors in all their wars; it is just so with the saints.] heaven and hell; like the tribe of Manasseh, half on this side of the Jordan, in the land of the Amorites, and half on that side, in the Holy Land. Yet in the end they will overcome the flesh and trample on the necks of their spiritual enemies.
